-- title:
--   Counit of a finite-part idempotent equals 1
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative local ring, with maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ denoted `IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R`, and let $H$ be a commutative ring carrying the structure of a Hopf algebra over $R$; write $\varepsilon =$ `Coalgebra.counit` for the counit, an $R$-linear map $H \to R$. Let $e \in H$ be an idempotent element, $e^2 = e$. Assume that the ideal of the away-localisation $H[1/(1-e)] =$ `Localization.Away (1 - e)` generated by the image of $\mathfrak m$ under the structure map $R \to H[1/(1-e)]$ is the whole ring, i.e. $\mathfrak m\, H[1/(1-e)] = H[1/(1-e)]$, so that the complementary corner of $H$ has empty closed fibre over $R$. The conclusion is that $\varepsilon(e) = 1$ in $R$. Note that no finiteness assumption on the corner $H[1/e]$ is made: the hypotheses on $e$ are idempotency together with the single condition that the maximal ideal of $R$ expands to the unit ideal in $H[1/(1-e)]$.
--
--   Geometrically, if $G = \operatorname{Spec} H$ is a commutative group scheme over the local ring $R$ and $e$ is the idempotent cutting out a "finite part" $G^{f}$ whose complement has empty special fibre, this says that the unit section of $G$ factors through $G^{f}$, i.e. $\varepsilon(e) = 1$. It is the basic normalisation used in the Hopf-algebra treatment of the finite-part splitting, and is invoked in the construction of the quotient Hopf algebra attached to $e$ and in the comparison of Hopf kernels under that splitting.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_HopfAlgebra_counit_finitePartIdempotent.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
